From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:40:38 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: read only the RX descriptor words
 used

On the MT7986 the RX rings are in the frame engine's SRAM, inside the
ioremap()ed register window: device memory, so each descriptor word
mtk_rx_get_desc() reads is a separate uncached bus read, which stalls
an in-order Cortex-A53. It read rxd1-rxd4 for every packet (rxd1-rxd7
on v3), but mtk_poll_rx() uses fewer:
- rxd1, the buffer address, only on the path without a page_pool
  (netsys v1; v2 and later always have one);
- rxd3 only on v1 (the VLAN port of a DSA frame) and v3 (checksum);
- rxd4 only up to v2 (source port, special tag, PPE reason, FOE entry,
  checksum), which rxd5 carries on v3;
- rxd6 and rxd7 not at all.
So read those only where they are used: on the MT7986, two reads per
packet (rxd2 and rxd4) instead of four.

Also add a dma_rmb() after the RX_DMA_DONE check, so that the other
words cannot be read before the ownership word on a ring in normal
memory (dma_alloc_coherent(), or an SRAM pool mapped write-combining).
Device memory is not read speculatively, so this changes nothing for
the SRAM rings here, apart from the cost of the barrier.

[Based on pesa1234 999-9919 (SmartWRT), which reads SRAM descriptors
 with two 64-bit readq_relaxed() instead, adds the dma_rmb() and stops
 reading rxd6/rxd7. Reworked and ported to OpenWrt 6.12 for MilkFlint
 by OddBlueDog: the same two reads per packet on the MT7986, but of
 the two words used and at the access width the driver already uses,
 for every ring (no in_sram path, nothing new for the bus); it holds
 for the four RSS rings, which are all in SRAM with a page_pool.]

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c | 31 ++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
index 560b056..f3f82aa 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -1358,20 +1358,37 @@ static inline int mtk_max_buf_size(int frag_size)
 	return buf_size;
 }
 
-static bool mtk_rx_get_desc(struct mtk_eth *eth, struct mtk_rx_dma_v2 *rxd,
+static bool mtk_rx_get_desc(struct mtk_eth *eth, struct mtk_rx_ring *ring,
+			    struct mtk_rx_dma_v2 *rxd,
 			    struct mtk_rx_dma_v2 *dma_rxd)
 {
 	rxd->rxd2 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd2);
 	if (!(rxd->rxd2 & RX_DMA_DONE))
 		return false;
 
-	rxd->rxd1 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd1);
-	rxd->rxd3 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd3);
-	rxd->rxd4 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd4);
+	/* Read the other words only after the DMA engine has handed the
+	 * descriptor back. A ring in the frame engine's SRAM is device
+	 * memory, which is never read speculatively, but a ring from
+	 * dma_alloc_coherent() is normal memory.
+	 */
+	dma_rmb();
+
+	/* Every word read from the frame engine's SRAM is an uncached bus
+	 * read: read only what mtk_poll_rx() uses. rxd1, the buffer
+	 * address, only without a page_pool; rxd3, the VLAN port on v1 and
+	 * the checksum on v3; rxd4 up to v2 and rxd5 on v3, the source
+	 * port, PPE reason and FOE entry (and the checksum on v1/v2).
+	 */
+	if (!ring->page_pool)
+		rxd->rxd1 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd1);
+
 	if (mtk_is_netsys_v3_or_greater(eth)) {
+		rxd->rxd3 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd3);
 		rxd->rxd5 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd5);
-		rxd->rxd6 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd6);
-		rxd->rxd7 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd7);
+	} else {
+		if (mtk_is_netsys_v1(eth))
+			rxd->rxd3 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd3);
+		rxd->rxd4 = READ_ONCE(dma_rxd->rxd4);
 	}
 
 	return true;
@@ -2441,7 +2458,7 @@ static int mtk_poll_rx(struct napi_struct *napi, int budget,
 		rxd = ring->dma + RX_DESC_OFS(eth, idx);
 		data = ring->data[idx];
 
-		if (!mtk_rx_get_desc(eth, &trxd, rxd))
+		if (!mtk_rx_get_desc(eth, ring, &trxd, rxd))
 			break;
 
 		/* find out which mac the packet come from. values start at 1 */
